Clear a stale error state on SCO transport acquire.

After an HFP session ends (for example, WirePlumber's autoswitch
restores A2DP once the last capture stream closes), sco_release_cb
moves the SCO transport from active to idle.  media-sink treats
active -> idle of a started, non-duplex output as a remote failure and
sets the transport state to error.  The SCO transport object survives
the profile switch, so that error state is still there on the next
switch to HFP.  When the second node acquires the already acquired
transport, spa_bt_transport_acquire() re-emits the stale state, both
nodes emit SPA_NODE_EVENT_Error, and the client's capture stream dies
after ~0.6 s.  The failed attempt resets the state, so every other
call works: automatic HFP switching fails exactly every second time.

Reset the error state when a new SCO acquire starts.  Real failures
during that acquire set it again.

Also backport the 1.6.11 change that clears the error counter after a
successful acquire, so a few quick HFP sessions in a row do not hit
TRANSPORT_ERROR_MAX_RETRY because of these release-time errors.

--- a/spa/plugins/bluez5/backend-native.c
+++ b/spa/plugins/bluez5/backend-native.c
@@ -2814,6 +2814,18 @@
 
 	spa_log_debug(backend->log, "transport %p: enter sco_acquire_cb", t);
 
+	/* The SCO transport outlives its nodes across profile switches, and
+	 * releasing a running sink marks it as failed (media-sink treats
+	 * active -> idle of a started output as an error).  Nothing resets
+	 * that state before the next acquire, so the next user's nodes see
+	 * the stale error and fail at once.  A new acquire is a new attempt:
+	 * start it from idle; real failures below set the error state again.
+	 */
+	if (t->state == SPA_BT_TRANSPORT_STATE_ERROR) {
+		spa_log_debug(backend->log, "transport %p: clearing stale error state", t);
+		spa_bt_transport_set_state(t, SPA_BT_TRANSPORT_STATE_IDLE);
+	}
+
 	if (optional || t->fd > 0)
 		sock = t->fd;
 	else
--- a/spa/plugins/bluez5/bluez5-dbus.c
+++ b/spa/plugins/bluez5/bluez5-dbus.c
@@ -3452,6 +3452,8 @@
 	if (res >= 0) {
 		transport->acquire_refcount = 1;
 		transport->acquired = true;
+		/* a transport that acquires is working */
+		transport->error_count = 0;
 	}
 
 	return res;
